WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.802
-Our first guests tonight
are two talented

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:05.004
and award-winning actors
who star in

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:06.673
"The Banshees of Inisherin,"

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:08.341
which will be in theaters
October 21st.

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:10.810
Please welcome back to the show,
two of my all-time favorites

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:13.746
here together, Brendan Gleeson
and Colin Farrell, everybody!

00:00:13.746 --> 00:00:15.248
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:25.258 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:25.258 --> 00:00:32.832 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:36.302
-Well, this is just lovely
to have you both here.

00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:38.370
-It's fantastic to be back.
-Hello, everyone.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:39.872
[ Cheers ]

00:00:39.872 --> 00:00:42.342
-You're both back on the show,
and, Brendan, just on Saturday,

00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:43.409
you're back in the building.

00:00:43.409 --> 00:00:45.277
You hosted "SNL"
right down the hall.

00:00:45.277 --> 00:00:48.214
Wonderful job.
-Thank you, this is weird.

00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:52.118
You know --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:52.118 --> 00:00:53.519
Coming in, I said,
"Wait a minute.

00:00:53.519 --> 00:00:56.021
Has it not happened?"
[ Laughter ]

00:00:56.021 --> 00:00:58.024
And we meet Donna outside,
and it's like,

00:00:58.024 --> 00:00:59.692
"Yeah, okay,
let's get back into this."

00:00:59.692 --> 00:01:02.361
-Yeah, exactly.
Now were you hesitant at all

00:01:02.361 --> 00:01:04.830
to take on
the hosting duties at "SNL"?

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:07.467
-Only to the degree
of saying no.

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:09.602
[ Laughter ]

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:11.871
-Was Colin helpful?
-Many times, by the way --

00:01:11.871 --> 00:01:13.973
many times he said no.
I was. Didn't I?

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:17.509
I sold it as hard as I could.
-Yeah, in my -- he said --

00:01:17.509 --> 00:01:19.412
-I said it was one of the best
weeks you'll ever have

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:21.147
that you'll learn
to regret eventually.

00:01:21.147 --> 00:01:22.248
[ Laughter ]

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:24.416
No, but I had --
it was 16, 18 --

00:01:24.416 --> 00:01:26.052
-It's a long time ago.
-A long, long time ago.

00:01:26.052 --> 00:01:27.886
And we were there together.
-Yeah, and I can tell

00:01:27.886 --> 00:01:29.889
it's a long time ago
because you look terrible.

00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:31.024
[ Laughter ]
-I look terrible.

00:01:31.024 --> 00:01:33.125
Yeah, exactly.
-You've aged so badly.

00:01:33.125 --> 00:01:35.929
It's all we talk about, Colin.
-I comb the hairline down

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:37.697
on the regular, man.
On the regular.

00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:40.066
I give myself that extra
half an inch, you know yourself.

00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:43.336
But, listen, no, it was one of
the best weeks I had creatively.

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:45.804
The chaos of 30 Rock on a
Saturday, which you lived in...

00:01:45.804 --> 00:01:47.173
-Yeah.
-...and it's so --

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:50.043
We get so much time to prepare.
Different in theater,

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:51.310
but when you're doing
television or film,

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:52.745
you have
so much time to prepare.

00:01:52.745 --> 00:01:55.281
You can't really fail too badly.

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:56.649
You can,
you just go back to one,

00:01:56.649 --> 00:01:58.284
the camera, you cut it,
or whatever.

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:00.119
This is all very different.
And "Saturday Night Live,"

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:05.191
the chaos, this kind of -- the
strategic chaos is so beautiful.

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:06.225
I knew he'd love it.

00:02:06.225 --> 00:02:07.426
-You got roped in
a couple of times.

00:02:07.426 --> 00:02:09.862
Was it less stressful?
-Totally!

00:02:09.862 --> 00:02:11.196
-It's wonderful to be there!
-Oh, I was sitting back

00:02:11.196 --> 00:02:13.499
in the room, eating the cookies,
and watching this fella

00:02:13.499 --> 00:02:16.702
be dragged by the --
you know, to his next costume

00:02:16.702 --> 00:02:18.504
or dodgy wig.
It was fantastic.

00:02:18.504 --> 00:02:20.005
[ Laughter ]
It was fantastic.

00:02:20.005 --> 00:02:23.308
-It's like nothing else, but it
did bring me back to the '80s.

00:02:23.308 --> 00:02:26.012
-Uh-huh.
-We used to do a lot of --

00:02:26.012 --> 00:02:29.916
a lot of running around
with kind of very large hoops

00:02:29.916 --> 00:02:33.686
and skirts and bad wigs.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:35.354
With a whole bunch of people

00:02:35.354 --> 00:02:36.789
with the Passion Machine
Theatre Company.

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:39.291
And we used to -- the audience
participation was the same,

00:02:39.291 --> 00:02:41.327
The sense of excitement.
I was running around the back

00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:44.063
of the sets, 'cause it's so
theatrical, the setup.

00:02:44.063 --> 00:02:45.665
-Yeah.
-And said, "I got this."

00:02:45.665 --> 00:02:46.999
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.999 --> 00:02:48.301
-Well, you certainly --
-Were you skating back then?

00:02:48.301 --> 00:02:49.702
Were you a skateboarder
back then, as well?

00:02:49.702 --> 00:02:51.036
-I was, yeah, yeah.

00:02:51.036 --> 00:02:52.305
-That's something
that came later in life.

00:02:52.305 --> 00:02:54.840
-Yeah, I had a stunt double
even then.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.643
-Even as young man.
You mentioned Donna.

00:02:57.643 --> 00:03:00.579
She's the dresser at "SNL."
She works here.

00:03:00.579 --> 00:03:02.381
Wally, our cue card guy,

00:03:02.381 --> 00:03:05.551
also was sort of your touchstone
during the week.

00:03:05.551 --> 00:03:07.653
And he mentioned
you did a scene where --

00:03:07.653 --> 00:03:10.189
the blood-oath scene, where you
were sort of spraying blood.

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:12.191
And Wally wanted to let us know,
he caught it, as well.

00:03:12.191 --> 00:03:14.260
He actually --
this is the actual cue card,

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.462
covered in blood.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:16.462 --> 00:03:19.532
-Thank you, Wally!
-Thank you, Wally.

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:21.901
-Oh, that was funny.
He said, "Yeah, don't --"

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:23.802
[ Laughter ]

00:03:23.802 --> 00:03:25.738
-Oh, very good.
Very good, Wally.

00:03:25.738 --> 00:03:27.840
-Yeah, "Oh, am I gettin' ya?"
[ Laughter ]

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.775
-Yeah, you seemed to be enjoying
getting everybody.

00:03:29.775 --> 00:03:31.311
-He got you, didn't he?
I saw you.

00:03:31.311 --> 00:03:34.080
-So this is --
talking about, I guess --

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:36.215
So "In Bruges" happened
after you hosted "SNL,"

00:03:36.215 --> 00:03:39.251
but an equally long time ago,
14 years ago you make this film.

00:03:39.251 --> 00:03:40.787
Martin McDonagh,
who wrote and directed this,

00:03:40.787 --> 00:03:42.555
comes back to you guys.
Was this --

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:45.090
Was there an expectation
you were gonna get the gang

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:47.393
back together at any point
over the last decade-plus?

00:03:47.393 --> 00:03:50.062
-We got on so well,
I always hoped.

00:03:50.062 --> 00:03:53.065
I've worked with Martin
twice before.

00:03:53.065 --> 00:03:56.302
My son had done a play of his.
So I knew his work.

00:03:56.302 --> 00:03:58.571
I'd say the best day of theater
I ever spent

00:03:58.571 --> 00:04:01.173
was three Martin McDonagh plays
in a row.

00:04:01.173 --> 00:04:02.975
Druid Theatre Company did it.

00:04:02.975 --> 00:04:04.243
-Which you were dreading
initially, weren't you?

00:04:04.243 --> 00:04:06.312
-I dreaded it.
I was like, "No, geez, ah."

00:04:06.312 --> 00:04:08.013
Existential.
-It seems like this is thematic

00:04:08.013 --> 00:04:10.783
that you just live in fear until
you decide to do something.

00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:12.117
[ Laughter ]

00:04:12.117 --> 00:04:14.854
-No, I did. I did it.
Then, it was one of the best.

00:04:14.854 --> 00:04:16.322
I was absolutely elated.

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:18.891
It kind of reawakened everything
that's brilliant about theater.

00:04:18.891 --> 00:04:20.592
But working with Martin
is extraordinary.

00:04:20.592 --> 00:04:23.029
And then we kind of hit it off
in "In Bruges."

00:04:23.029 --> 00:04:24.496
Col came in,
we just kind of hooked up.

00:04:24.496 --> 00:04:25.598
It was kind of immediate.

00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:26.899
There was no need for words,
really.

00:04:26.899 --> 00:04:28.401
-Hooked up, just to be clear...
-Oh, yeah.

00:04:28.401 --> 00:04:29.802
[ Laughter ]

00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:33.272
-Being in America, I feel like
a translation is in order.

00:04:33.272 --> 00:04:36.309
-Yeah.
-It didn't work.

00:04:36.309 --> 00:04:38.211
-I apologize.
-Leave them wanting more.

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:40.513
[ Laughter ]

00:04:40.513 --> 00:04:43.516
-No, it was fantastic.
So it was something

00:04:43.516 --> 00:04:45.818
that Martin was thinking
of getting the gang together

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:48.121
at some point.
But like 14 years.

00:04:48.121 --> 00:04:49.988
Seven years ago, I think,
the first iteration,

00:04:49.988 --> 00:04:51.757
and then another couple --
so about three years ago

00:04:51.757 --> 00:04:54.360
it all started to be real.
And it was really exciting.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:58.597
And then COVID came in, and,
you know, "Is it gonna happen?"

00:04:58.597 --> 00:05:01.667
And then it happened, so it was
really -- it was fantastic.

00:05:01.667 --> 00:05:03.202
It was a dream.
A dream.

00:05:03.202 --> 00:05:05.905
-Did you find that you fell back
into it right away?

00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:07.306
-Straight away.
Nothing.

00:05:07.306 --> 00:05:09.409
I mean, I was a little bit
nervous because our characters

00:05:09.409 --> 00:05:11.677
in this film,
"The Banshees of Inisherin,"

00:05:11.677 --> 00:05:14.546
are at loggerheads, you know?

00:05:14.546 --> 00:05:16.448
Loggerheads.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:16.448 --> 00:05:18.484
-Ha ha, loggerheads!

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:19.752
-Where's Freud
when you need him?

00:05:19.752 --> 00:05:20.853
[ Laughter ]

00:05:20.853 --> 00:05:22.221
To stay quiet.

00:05:22.221 --> 00:05:24.089
Where is he when you need him
to stay quiet.

00:05:24.089 --> 00:05:25.224
-"Hooked up,"
and all of a sudden

00:05:25.224 --> 00:05:26.459
you come out with "loggerheads."

00:05:26.459 --> 00:05:28.594
-The characters
are at bourbonheads.

00:05:28.594 --> 00:05:32.498
And you know, "In Bruges"
was about our two characters

00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:33.865
falling in love with each other.
-Yeah.

00:05:33.865 --> 00:05:36.269
-You know, with all this madness
and chaos going around,

00:05:36.269 --> 00:05:39.204
and a friendship
that was being built

00:05:39.204 --> 00:05:43.475
against even our awareness.
And this is about both of us

00:05:43.475 --> 00:05:46.145
being keenly aware that it's the
dissolution of a friendship.

00:05:46.145 --> 00:05:49.615
So we -- I'm only happy for the
first 30 seconds of the film.

00:05:49.615 --> 00:05:50.650
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:52.018
-You really are.
-And then he tells me --

00:05:52.018 --> 00:05:54.019
And I'm really happy, by the
way, in that 30 seconds.

00:05:54.019 --> 00:05:55.488
-Yeah.
-You know, it had to last

00:05:55.488 --> 00:05:57.256
the rest of the 90 minutes.

00:05:57.256 --> 00:05:58.457
And then he tells me
he doesn't want to be friends

00:05:58.457 --> 00:06:00.093
with me anymore.
So it was this dismantling

00:06:00.093 --> 00:06:01.660
of stuff, so I wasn't sure.

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:03.596
Me and him had a chat
the first day of rehearsal.

00:06:03.596 --> 00:06:06.198
"Look, are we gonna have to
not be fully in character

00:06:06.198 --> 00:06:07.967
or not talk,
but do we want to kind of --

00:06:07.967 --> 00:06:09.302
-Keep a distance.

00:06:09.302 --> 00:06:12.205
-And thank God, neither of us
felt we needed it.

00:06:12.205 --> 00:06:13.305
[ Laughter ]

00:06:13.305 --> 00:06:14.673
-Yeah, you didn't
want to go that method.

00:06:14.673 --> 00:06:17.376
-Do you know what I mean?
No, no, we don't need that.

00:06:17.376 --> 00:06:18.944
We didn't need it.
It's sometimes better, you know,

00:06:18.944 --> 00:06:20.079
you come from a place of love,

00:06:20.079 --> 00:06:22.081
and then you can
take it down consciously.

00:06:22.081 --> 00:06:24.349
Take it apart consciously
and still look after each other.

00:06:24.349 --> 00:06:27.053
'Cause some of the stuff
was very dark in this, as well.

00:06:27.053 --> 00:06:29.288
Same as "Bruges," some of it.
It's hilarious when you

00:06:29.288 --> 00:06:31.924
read it first,
and it's funny when people

00:06:31.924 --> 00:06:34.326
watch it, apparently.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:34.326 --> 00:06:35.895
But there's a middle section
of doing it

00:06:35.895 --> 00:06:36.995
that can get
a bit tricky sometimes.

00:06:36.995 --> 00:06:37.930
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:39.398
-You know?
-Yeah.

00:06:39.398 --> 00:06:41.333
-Now you guys might have heard
that I'm a fan of "In Bruges."

00:06:41.333 --> 00:06:42.768
-A little.
-I've heard that.

00:06:42.768 --> 00:06:44.537
-We've talked about that
a fair amount on the show.

00:06:44.537 --> 00:06:46.672
And I don't know --
I feel like I didn't really

00:06:46.672 --> 00:06:49.341
fully admit this to you, Colin.
Because I mentioned once that

00:06:49.341 --> 00:06:51.077
I had told my wife
I wanted to go to Paris,

00:06:51.077 --> 00:06:53.078
and then I tricked her
into going to "In Bruges."

00:06:53.078 --> 00:06:55.948
[ Laughter ]
-Good trick.

00:06:55.948 --> 00:06:58.551
-I don't think you know
the extent that we went.

00:06:58.551 --> 00:07:00.719
We tried to re-create
frames of the film.

00:07:00.719 --> 00:07:03.656 line:0%
So, like, there's you,
in the movie, and there's me.

00:07:03.656 --> 00:07:05.491 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:07:05.491 --> 00:07:07.960 line:0%
And so there, Brendan, don't
think you -- there you are.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.996 line:0%
-Oh, yeah!
-And there you are, there I am.

00:07:10.996 --> 00:07:13.799 line:0%
-Your poor wife.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:07:13.799 --> 00:07:16.101 line:0%
You have no idea.
-Was she a director?

00:07:16.101 --> 00:07:18.737 line:0%
-There's Ralph Fiennes,
and there I am walking across --

00:07:18.737 --> 00:07:20.172 line:0%
You think "poor wife"?

00:07:20.172 --> 00:07:21.974 line:0%
You want to know how bad it is
for my wife?

00:07:21.974 --> 00:07:23.909 line:0%
We didn't have a dog with us.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:23.909 --> 00:07:26.678 line:0%
-Oh, no!
-This gorgeous woman.

00:07:26.678 --> 00:07:28.014
[ Applause ]
-Beautiful.

00:07:28.014 --> 00:07:32.284
-That gorgeous woman...
-Beautiful.

00:07:32.284 --> 00:07:34.787
-So I was very excited because,
you know, obviously,

00:07:34.787 --> 00:07:36.922
my expectation
in having loved the film,

00:07:36.922 --> 00:07:41.293
I intend to, as gorgeous a place
as you could shoot this.

00:07:41.293 --> 00:07:42.628
-Are you gonna go --
Did you ever?

00:07:42.628 --> 00:07:44.096
-I mean,
I would love to go to --

00:07:44.096 --> 00:07:45.131
Now, where was actually
Inisherin?

00:07:45.131 --> 00:07:46.432
-Well, that's the thing.
Go where?

00:07:46.432 --> 00:07:47.566
So Inisherin
doesn't actually exist.

00:07:47.566 --> 00:07:48.734
It's not a place.

00:07:48.734 --> 00:07:49.835
-So just get there
and keep going.

00:07:49.835 --> 00:07:51.571
-A mythological isle.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:07:51.571 --> 00:07:53.205
Anywhere on the west coast,
really.

00:07:53.205 --> 00:07:54.340
We shot on two islands.

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:56.274
One was one of the three islands

00:07:56.274 --> 00:07:57.776
that constitute
the Aran Islands,

00:07:57.776 --> 00:07:59.344
and the island
that we shot on was Inishmore,

00:07:59.344 --> 00:08:00.947
the biggest of the islands,
the biggest of the three.

00:08:00.947 --> 00:08:02.281
And was just extraordinary,

00:08:02.281 --> 00:08:04.716
and it has a population
of 750 people.

00:08:04.716 --> 00:08:06.118
On any given day
during the summer,

00:08:06.118 --> 00:08:07.753
2,000 tourists come and go.

00:08:07.753 --> 00:08:09.788
So it's abuzz during the day,
and then, at night,

00:08:09.788 --> 00:08:12.024
it becomes a more sincere
version of itself, in a way.

00:08:12.024 --> 00:08:13.826
A magic, magic place.
It doesn't feel

00:08:13.826 --> 00:08:16.329
like it's changed too much
in forever, you know?

00:08:16.329 --> 00:08:17.963
-And we were kind of embraced
on it, in a way that was...

00:08:17.963 --> 00:08:19.565
-Yeah.
-...just total.

00:08:19.565 --> 00:08:24.236
So, you know, like, there's
quite a toxic environment

00:08:24.236 --> 00:08:26.605
that can develop on the island
in the movie.

00:08:26.605 --> 00:08:28.774
Not at all
what happened on Inishmore.

00:08:28.774 --> 00:08:30.742
They were just --
They took us in,

00:08:30.742 --> 00:08:32.344
and we'd kind of pass each other
on the road

00:08:32.344 --> 00:08:33.946
in the way it was happening
in the film.

00:08:33.946 --> 00:08:35.581
It was such a crossover.

00:08:35.581 --> 00:08:37.616
-"How are you, Brendan?"
[ Laughter ]

00:08:37.616 --> 00:08:40.119
-So between that
and Achill Island,

00:08:40.119 --> 00:08:42.021
which was the other place
we had locations,

00:08:42.021 --> 00:08:43.189
they're magnificent places.

00:08:43.189 --> 00:08:44.657
And we got
extraordinary weather.

00:08:44.657 --> 00:08:47.159
It was like as if they wanted us
to make this movie over there.

00:08:47.159 --> 00:08:50.596
-And you -- there are a great
many animals in the film.

00:08:50.596 --> 00:08:53.565
Are they local hires or did you
bring them over with you?

00:08:53.565 --> 00:08:55.000
-They were definitely
local hires.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:56.101
They were first-timers.

00:08:56.101 --> 00:08:57.903
Yeah, but they were just
extraordinary.

00:08:57.903 --> 00:09:00.906
That looks like CGI to me,
but they were there, I remember.

00:09:00.906 --> 00:09:02.508
[ Laughter ]

00:09:02.508 --> 00:09:03.675
Extraordinary.
-Yeah.

00:09:03.675 --> 00:09:06.178
And did you find --
I mean, you --

00:09:06.178 --> 00:09:09.715
I feel like you do the brunt
of the animal acting

00:09:09.715 --> 00:09:10.849
in the film, Colin...
-Yeah.

00:09:10.849 --> 00:09:12.285
-...and I feel like
you did a great job.

00:09:12.285 --> 00:09:14.920
I felt like you really had a
connection with those animals.

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:17.623
-The animals had a connection
with me, didn't they?

00:09:17.623 --> 00:09:19.625
One connected
its hoof to my kneecap.

00:09:19.625 --> 00:09:20.859
[ Laughter ]

00:09:20.859 --> 00:09:22.962
The other one connected
its teeth to my thumb.

00:09:22.962 --> 00:09:24.797
[ Audience groans ]
True.

00:09:24.797 --> 00:09:26.065
[ Laughter ]

00:09:26.065 --> 00:09:29.234
There's three animals,
outside the human animals,

00:09:29.234 --> 00:09:30.569
three animals that are, like,

00:09:30.569 --> 00:09:34.006
livestock or domesticated
animals that we worked with.

00:09:34.006 --> 00:09:37.876
There was Morse, who was this
gorgeous sheep dog.

00:09:37.876 --> 00:09:39.712
Gorgeous.
-Beautiful dog.

00:09:39.712 --> 00:09:41.847
-Smart, wise,
terrible judge of character.

00:09:41.847 --> 00:09:43.849
It bit me.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:43.849 --> 00:09:47.019
-It did not like to dance.
-Did not like to dance.

00:09:47.019 --> 00:09:48.654
-You do have a dancing scene
with a sheep dog.

00:09:48.654 --> 00:09:49.622
-I do.
-Yeah.

00:09:49.622 --> 00:09:51.257
-I do!
It was emotional.

00:09:51.257 --> 00:09:53.459
[ Laughter ]

00:09:53.459 --> 00:09:55.494
-Jenny --
Jenny the donkey.

00:09:55.494 --> 00:09:57.930
And she gave me
a good ol' clatter on the knee.

00:09:57.930 --> 00:09:58.930
[ Laughter ]

00:09:58.930 --> 00:10:01.567
And then --
And then Minnie,

00:10:01.567 --> 00:10:03.735
who didn't have a big part
but then kind of

00:10:03.735 --> 00:10:06.405
worked her way up
the affections of the director,

00:10:06.405 --> 00:10:08.174
at the writer/director Martin.

00:10:08.174 --> 00:10:10.476
And he ended up giving her a lot
more than she originally had.

00:10:10.476 --> 00:10:12.344
-Yeah, 'cause originally --
-To the betterment of the film

00:10:12.344 --> 00:10:15.113
but to the detriment
of my safety because --

00:10:15.113 --> 00:10:17.316
because she wouldn't
stay to your right.

00:10:17.316 --> 00:10:19.485
Do you remember the scene,
Barry with the kettle?

00:10:19.485 --> 00:10:21.153
-I do.
-On the way to the church.

00:10:21.153 --> 00:10:22.388
Do you remember the drop-off
there at the cliff?

00:10:22.388 --> 00:10:24.156
-I do.
-That's where she starts

00:10:24.156 --> 00:10:26.592
to back up towards the cliff.
[ Laughter ]

00:10:26.592 --> 00:10:28.327
And the bit that we were doing,
as well, that day,

00:10:28.327 --> 00:10:29.995
she was a bit --
-She was a little bit --

00:10:29.995 --> 00:10:32.464
Yeah, she was a bit diva-ish.
[ Laughter ]

00:10:32.464 --> 00:10:34.199
-Colin, I have to say,
are you worried at all

00:10:34.199 --> 00:10:36.535
about the fact that, you know,
three different animal species

00:10:36.535 --> 00:10:39.871
seemed to chose to attack you
over Brendan?

00:10:39.871 --> 00:10:42.308
-Yeah, it was concerning.
[ Laughter ]

00:10:42.308 --> 00:10:44.243
-Well, Colin --
-I still have no answer for it.

00:10:44.243 --> 00:10:46.445
-I think, you know the way you
always hurt the one you love?

00:10:46.445 --> 00:10:48.814
-Yeah.
-Oh, I like that.

00:10:48.814 --> 00:10:51.717
-What a nice framing of it.

00:10:51.717 --> 00:10:52.885
I have a lot more
to ask you guys.

00:10:52.885 --> 00:10:54.086
We'll be right back with more

00:10:54.086 --> 00:10:55.788
from Brendan Gleeson
and Colin Farrell.

